    <main id="main" class="page-width article">
      <header class="article-intro">
        <p class="location">White Plains, New York</p>
        <h1>How Westminster Ridge got its lake</h1>
        <p class="intro">A hundred and twenty years ago this hillside was one man's land: open fields and woods, with no streets and no lake. This is the story of how it became a neighborhood, told through the maps, magazines, and photographs that survive.</p>
        <figure class="figure figure-wide">
          <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the 1910 map in a larger view">
            <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1910-white-plains-map.jpg" width="1536" height="999" alt="1910 map of White Plains and the surrounding area" />
            <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
          </button>
          <figcaption>White Plains in 1910. Westminster Ridge would grow up near the eastern edge of this map. <span class="credit">Westchester County atlas.</span></figcaption>
        </figure>
      </header>

      <section id="land" class="story-section" aria-labelledby="land-title">
        <h2 id="land-title">Mr. Todd's land</h2>
        <p>For most of its history White Plains was farm country. The Weckquaesgeek called the area Quarropas, usually translated as "white marshes." English settlers bought the land in 1683, and as late as 1820 only about 675 people lived here. That began to change in 1844, when the railroad arrived from New York City.</p>
        <p>By 1901 the hillside that would become Westminster Ridge belonged to A. C. Todd. The county atlas from that year prints his name across a large tract of open land. There are no streets on it yet, and no lake.</p>
        <p>Todd was not a farmer. He was a Manhattan businessman who dealt in real estate from an office at 92 Spring Street. In 1905 his A. C. Todd Realty Company was running small ads in <em>The New York Times</em> offering hundreds of "building lots for sale at White Plains."</p>
        <div class="image-grid image-grid-2">
          <figure class="figure">
            <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the 1901 A. C. Todd map in a larger view">
              <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1901-ac-todd.jpg" width="1200" height="1120" alt="1901 county atlas detail labeled A. C. Todd" loading="lazy" />
              <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
            </button>
            <figcaption>1901. The tract is marked "A. C. Todd." No lake appears. <span class="credit">Westchester County Archives.</span></figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="figure">
            <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the 1911 reservoir map in a larger view">
              <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1911-reservoir.jpg" width="1800" height="1250" alt="1911 county atlas showing a reservoir and a dam-like feature" loading="lazy" />
              <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
            </button>
            <figcaption>1911. A "RESERVOIR" has appeared, with a dam drawn at one end. <span class="credit">Westchester County Archives.</span></figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p>By 1910 Todd had drawn a subdivision on the hill and named it Westminster Park. A Bromley atlas from that year shows the whole plan: Colonial Terrace looping up the hill, Gilbert Place across the middle, Garretson Road along the bottom, and every lot numbered. Only a handful of buildings stood on it. At the foot of the hill, on land left out of the lots, is a long pond labeled "RESERVOIR." North of the tract, the map marks a large area as the "White Plains Water Shed."</p>
        <figure class="figure figure-wide">
          <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the 1910 Westminster Park atlas plate in a larger view">
            <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1910-westminster-park.jpg" width="1060" height="860" alt="1910 atlas plate showing Westminster Park lots, Colonial Terrace, Gilbert Place, Garretson Road, and the reservoir" loading="lazy" />
            <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
          </button>
          <figcaption>Westminster Park in 1910. Pink and yellow shapes are buildings; almost every lot is still empty. <span class="credit">G. W. Bromley &amp; Co., Atlas of Westchester County, 1910. David Rumsey Map Collection.</span></figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>People were already calling the water Todd's Pond. On October 7, 1913, the entomologist J. R. de la Torre-Bueno spent a warm autumn day turning over stones "on the hill-side by Todd's Pond" and wrote down the insects he found. He came back to "the Todd hillside" in 1917. Years later he remembered collecting on the "sloping, grassy, weedy north shore of the pond, in what is now Westminster Ridge" with a friend who ate a caterpillar there and claimed to know "the taste of 300 kinds of caterpillars."</p>
        <p>We still don't know who dug or dammed the pond, or whether it began as a spring, a stream, or a wet meadow. But it was there before almost any of the houses.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="park" class="story-section" aria-labelledby="park-title">
        <h2 id="park-title">Westminster Park goes to auction</h2>
        <p>Lots sold slowly. In May 1916 Todd hired the Manhattan auctioneer Bryan L. Kennelly to sell "116 villa plots and 100 building lots in Westminster Park" in a single afternoon. The sale was held on Saturday, May 20, "under mammoth tent, rain or shine," on Orchard Street. There was a free bus or taxi from the White Plains station, and buyers could pay "part cash and small monthly payments."</p>
        <figure class="figure figure-wide">
          <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the 1916 Westminster Park auction advertisement in a larger view">
            <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1916-auction-ad.jpg" width="720" height="415" alt="1916 newspaper advertisement for the auction of 116 villa plots and 100 building lots in Westminster Park" loading="lazy" />
            <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
          </button>
          <figcaption>The auction advertisement. <span class="credit">The New York Times, May 7, 1916.</span></figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>The next year a builder tried a bigger plan. In January 1917 a New York real-estate journal announced 52 houses at Westminster Park, at a total cost of about $200,000. In July it listed the first one: a two-and-a-half-story frame and stucco house costing $10,000. Norman W. Walker was president of the Westminster Park Company, Joseph J. Eberle was the architect, and M. W. Walker was the builder.</p>
        <div class="image-grid image-grid-2">
          <figure class="figure">
            <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the January 1917 Westminster Park notice in a larger view">
              <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1917-january-notice.jpg" width="580" height="710" alt="January 1917 real-estate notice for Westminster Park" loading="lazy" />
              <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
            </button>
            <figcaption>January 1917: 52 houses planned. <span class="credit">Real Estate Record and Builders' Guide.</span></figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="figure">
            <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the July 1917 Westminster Park notice in a larger view">
              <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1917-july-notice.jpg" width="592" height="850" alt="July 1917 real-estate notice naming Westminster Park participants" loading="lazy" />
              <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
            </button>
            <figcaption>July 1917: the first house, and the men building it. <span class="credit">Real Estate Record and Builders' Guide.</span></figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p>Three months after the first notice, the United States entered the First World War, and building slowed across the country. We don't know how many of the 52 houses went up. Todd kept selling. In 1919 a sale map titled "Subdivision of property of A. C. Todd" was filed with the county, and his ads in the <em>Times</em> offered "lots, plots, acreage" at Westminster Park, "near depot." As late as 1921 he was still selling single plots on Hall and Woodland Avenues.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="stafford" class="story-section" aria-labelledby="stafford-title">
        <h2 id="stafford-title">Fred Stafford's Westminster Ridge</h2>
        <p>The next developer was Fred P. Stafford, the president of a local laundry and a national leader in Sunday-school work. Around 1927 he began selling the hill under a new name, Westminster Ridge. He kept six lots at the top for his own house, on Colonial Terrace. Roads went in over the next two years, following the slope of the land instead of a grid.</p>
        <p>Stafford sold the neighborhood hard. His 1929 ads in the <em>Times</em> offered "large wooded plots with fine views, $2,300 up," a "private lake, year-round sports," and an easy walk to the express train. Anyone in Manhattan could "see Westminster Ridge in miniature" as a model at his office on East 42nd Street. The ads also promised that "restrictions insure good neighbors." Deed restrictions in suburbs of this period set minimum house costs and often barred buyers by race or religion; the Westminster Ridge deeds themselves have not yet been checked.</p>
        <p>By January 1930, 28 houses had been built and all but three were sold. The <em>Times</em> listed some of the first owners: Charles W. Fowle, Frederick M. Holbrook, Wells F. Wise, W. C. Collins, William R. Foster, and Frank B. Madden. The houses sold for roughly $15,000 to $40,000. The same article said the new George Washington School stood at the entrance to the property, and that a "Colonial type community house" was about to be built on the shore of the lake.</p>
        <figure class="figure figure-half">
          <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the photograph of the Holbrook garden pool in a larger view">
            <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1932-holbrook-pool.jpg" width="600" height="745" alt="Water lilies in a stone garden pool with a hillside and houses behind it" loading="lazy" />
            <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
          </button>
          <figcaption>Tropical water lilies in the garden pool of Mrs. F. M. Holbrook, Westminster Ridge, about 1932. The photo won a prize at the International Flower Show. It is the earliest ground-level photograph of the neighborhood we have found. <span class="credit">Photo by Walter P. Daniels. William Tricker, Inc. catalog, 1932.</span></figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>In 1934 the city renamed Colonial Place in Stafford's honor. It has been Stafford Place ever since.</p>
        <p>According to a 1939 magazine article, the property "was once an estate," and the original house was still standing. Nobody has yet proven which house that is. The strongest candidate is 20 Stafford Place, a large stucco Colonial on nearly three-quarters of an acre. Property records date it to 1907, and it is on the street named for Stafford.</p>
        <figure class="figure figure-wide">
          <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the map showing 20 Stafford Place in a larger view">
            <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/20-stafford-map-lead.jpg" width="1800" height="1600" alt="Map showing an approximate marker near 20 Stafford Place and the reservoir" loading="lazy" />
            <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
          </button>
          <figcaption>The red marker shows where 20 Stafford Place sits today. We added it; it is not on the original map.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section id="perry" class="story-section" aria-labelledby="perry-title">
        <h2 id="perry-title">The Depression, and a second start</h2>
        <p>Then the market collapsed. In June 1930 the last 80 empty lots went up for auction, again under a large tent, "rain or shine." Few, if any, sold: eight years later, 80 lots were still empty. In 1936 another company filed plans to build on 95 lots at Westminster Ridge, but nothing came of it. In the two years before 1938, only two or three lots sold. The roads and utilities were in, but the hill had stopped filling up.</p>
        <p>In 1938 an architect and builder named Lee Perry bought all 80 lots through his firm, the County Housing Corporation. His idea was to build good houses that cost much less than Stafford's. "I can see no reason," he told <em>American Builder</em>, "why the prospective home owner considering a house worth only $6,000 is not entitled to as much architectural consideration as though he were planning a house costing $40,000."</p>
        <p>The owners of the big houses did not like the idea. According to <em>Architectural Forum</em>, they "feared undesirable neighbors" and fought to keep the rules that blocked cheaper homes. They lost, and the magazine said the ill feeling faded once the new families moved in.</p>
        <figure class="figure figure-half">
          <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the 1939 Westminster Ridge plot plan in a larger view">
            <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1939-plot-plan.jpg" width="467" height="570" alt="1939 plot plan showing winding roads, homes, woods, and the lake" loading="lazy" />
            <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
          </button>
          <figcaption>Perry's plan for Westminster Ridge, with the lake at the center. <span class="credit">Architectural Forum, March 1939.</span></figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Perry drew every house himself and fitted each one to its lot. On the steep sites he put the dormers on the side facing the view rather than the street. The first house went up in April 1938. By September, 41 were finished or under construction, and sales were running ahead of the builders. His ads said a house could be had for "as little as $598 cash and $48 per month."</p>
        <p id="house">Buyers chose from a few standard designs. The Lee was the smallest, with a downstairs lavatory and good closets. The Greenfield had five rooms, a dining alcove, and an attached garage. The Westminster was designed for the wooded hillside lots, and the Williamsburg had built-in seats at the entrance and a porch at the back. Prices ran from $5,800 to $8,500. <em>American Builder</em> later reprinted the Greenfield in a national plan book for builders.</p>
        <div class="image-grid image-grid-2">
          <figure class="figure">
            <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the 1938 house types page in a larger view">
              <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1938-house-types.jpg" width="1172" height="730" alt="1938 page showing Westminster Ridge house photographs and floor plans" loading="lazy" />
              <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
            </button>
            <figcaption>The Lee, The Westminster, and The Greenfield, with floor plans. <span class="credit">American Builder, November 1938.</span></figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="figure">
            <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the 1938 house photographs in a larger view">
              <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1938-houses.jpg" width="1172" height="520" alt="1938 photographs of two Westminster Ridge houses" loading="lazy" />
              <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
            </button>
            <figcaption>Two new houses in 1938, priced at $5,975 and $7,950. <span class="credit">American Builder, November 1938.</span></figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p>Every house was heated by gas. That caught the attention of the American Gas Association, which made Westminster Ridge a showplace in its national campaign for "all-gas" homes. Two demonstration houses opened to the public on September 15, 1938, with newspaper advertising paid for by the equipment makers. To trade on the neighborhood's good name, Perry called his next project, in New Rochelle, Westminster Acres.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="lake" class="story-section" aria-labelledby="lake-title">
        <h2 id="lake-title">The neighbors buy the lake</h2>
        <p>The lake had been a selling point from the start. In 1930 Stafford's residents were already swimming in it in summer and holding skating parties in winter, and the developers advertised a children's playground, the lake, and a community house. Perry's 1938 ads promised "a small lake and playground" as well. But the lake still belonged to the original developers.</p>
        <p>So the residents formed a lake club and set out to buy it themselves. In 1939 membership cost $2 a month. The dues paid for equipment, sand for the beach, and a lifeguard. Admission fees went toward the purchase price. It is the earliest record we have of what is now the Lakenridge Club.</p>
        <p>A county aerial photograph taken on December 19, 1940 shows the result: a finished neighborhood of curving streets and new roofs, with the pond in the middle.</p>
        <div class="image-grid image-grid-2">
          <figure class="figure">
            <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the 1940 aerial photograph in a larger view">
              <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1940-aerial.jpg" width="900" height="990" alt="1940 aerial photograph showing the pond among neighborhood roads and houses" loading="lazy" />
              <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
            </button>
            <figcaption>December 1940, from the air. <span class="credit">Westchester County historical aerial collection.</span></figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="figure">
            <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the Lakenridge Club letter in a larger view">
              <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/lakenridge-letter.png" width="935" height="1210" alt="Lakenridge Club membership letter describing the lake season" loading="lazy" />
              <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
            </button>
            <figcaption>A recent Lakenridge membership letter. Swimming, fishing, and the spring cleanup are still the center of the season.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p>By the 1950s the club was called Lakenridge; one resident's <em>Who's Who in America</em> entry lists a seat on its board. It received its federal tax-exempt status as a recreation club in 1973. Pasquale "Pat" Barile (1930–2020), a consulting engineer and White Plains civic leader, served as its president for many years. Today it is still run by neighbors. It hires lifeguards, runs a swim team and a kids' fishing derby, and opens each season with a lake cleanup.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="streets" class="story-section" aria-labelledby="streets-title">
        <h2 id="streets-title">The streets that were never built</h2>
        <p>Old maps of the neighborhood show more streets than exist today. The 1929–31 county atlas includes Bernard Place and Francis Place north of the pond, and a road called Stang Avenue running north from Colonial Place.</p>
        <div class="image-grid image-grid-2">
          <figure class="figure">
            <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the 1929 to 1931 street atlas in a larger view">
              <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1930-reservoir.jpg" width="2600" height="2300" alt="1929 to 1931 county atlas showing Bernard Place, Francis Place, Colonial Place, Stang Avenue, and the reservoir" loading="lazy" />
              <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
            </button>
            <figcaption>1929–31. The planned streets around the reservoir. <span class="credit">Westchester County Archives.</span></figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="figure">
            <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the present-day street map in a larger view">
              <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/today-pond-map.png" width="768" height="768" alt="Present-day map showing Chadwick Road, Stafford Place, Gilbert Place, and Todds Pond" loading="lazy" />
              <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
            </button>
            <figcaption>The same area today. Bernard Place and Francis Place are gone.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p>Most of the old names are still here under new ones. Colonial Place was built around 1908. In 1934 it was briefly called Chadwick Place, then renamed Stafford Place that September. The road now called Chadwick Road started as Colonial Terrace. Its northern extension, added around 1927, was first named Stang Avenue. In 1934 the whole road became Chadwick Road.</p>
        <p>Bernard Place and Francis Place were never built. They stayed on the city map as "paper streets," and Chadwick Road stops short of where Bernard Place would have been. In 2012 the city moved to buy the woods between Chadwick and Bernard as a wildlife corridor and connected them to city-owned land along Francis Place. The streets that were never built became open space instead.</p>
        <p class="source-line">Street history from <a href="https://wpstreets.weebly.com/home/stafford-place" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Streets of White Plains ↗</a> and the <a href="https://dailyvoice.com/article/white-plains-to-buy-wildlife-corridor-for-130k/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Daily Voice ↗</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="people" class="story-section" aria-labelledby="people-title">
        <h2 id="people-title">Who grew up here</h2>
        <p>Most of the people who lived on the ridge left little public record, but a few did. Stephen Holden III was born in White Plains and raised in Westminster Ridge. He became a local lawyer and civic leader who worked for historic preservation in the city. In 2009 the New York State Senate passed a resolution honoring his work, and it mentions the neighborhood by name.</p>
        <figure class="figure figure-half">
          <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the resolution honoring Stephen Holden III in a larger view">
            <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/holden-resolution.jpg" width="910" height="690" alt="2009 legislative resolution honoring Stephen Holden III" loading="lazy" />
            <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
          </button>
          <figcaption>The 2009 Senate resolution for Stephen Holden III. <span class="credit">New York State Senate.</span></figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>If you have old photographs of the pond, the beach, or the houses going up, or stories from people who remember the early lake club, we would like to hear from you. Much of this history is still in attics and family albums.</p>
      </section>

